on_status,\n          cd_purchase_estimate,\n          cd_credit_rating\n order by cd_gender,\n          cd_marital_status,\n          cd_education_status,\n          cd_purchase_estimate,\n          cd_credit_rating\n limit 100;\n```\n\nQuery Rewrite Strategies:\nQuery Rewrite 1:\n"""Using the matched cases to explain the SQL query changes:\n\nThe original query contains a scalar sub-query within the WHERE clause, which is transformed into a LEFT JOIN operation with an aggregate function on the column(s) being selected in the sub-query. The JOIN condition uses the correlation ID (the matching column(s) in both outer and sub-query).\n\nThe rewritten logical plan shows that the EXISTS sub-queries are converted to LEFT JOIN operations, and the IN sub-queries are replaced with INNER JOIN operations.\n\nFor example, the original query contains an EXISTS sub-query within the WHERE clause:\n\n```sql\nexists (select * from store_sales,date_dim where c.c_customer_sk = ss_customer_sk and ss_sold_date_sk = d_date_sk and d_year = 1999 and d_moy between 7 and 7+2 and ss_list_price between 139 and 228)\n```\n\nThis sub-query is rewritten as a LEFT JOIN operation:\n\n```sql\nLogicalCorrelate(correlation=[$cor0], joinType=[left], requiredColumns=[{0}])\n  LogicalFilter(condition=[AND(=($cor0.c_customer_sk, $3(ss_customer_sk)), =($0(ss_sold_date_sk), $23(d_date_sk)), =($29(d_year), 1999), >=($31(d_moy), 7), <=($31(d_moy), +(7, 2)), >=($12(ss_list_price), 139), <=($12(ss_list_price), 228))])\n```\n\nSimilarly, the IN sub-queries are rewritten as INNER JOIN operations:\n\n```sql\nLogicalFilter(condition=[AND(=($cor0.c_customer_sk, $3(ws_bill_customer_sk)), =($0(ws_sold_date_sk), $34(d_date_sk)), =($40(d_year), 1999), >=($42(d_moy), 7), <=($42(d_moy), +(7, 2)), >=($20(ws_list_price), 139), <=($20(ws_list_price), 228))])\n```\n\nThis rewritten logical plan shows that the query rewrite rule is applied to the original query, transforming the EXISTS and IN sub-queries into LEFT JOIN and INNER JOIN operations, respectively."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 2:\n"""The rewritten query matches Case 1 of the query rewrite rule. The original query has a `GROUP BY` clause that groups by simple columns (cd_gender, cd_marital_status, etc.), and the aggregate functions in the `SELECT` clause operate directly on these columns without any expressions inside the aggregate functions. Additionally, the `SELECT` clause only contains the columns used in the `GROUP BY` clause and the aggregates without any transformations or additional expressions.\n\nThe query rewrite rule applies to the given SQL query by simplifying the complex `SELECT` clause and eliminating the outer projection layer. This rewriting process allows the query to be executed more efficiently, as it eliminates unnecessary layers of processing and reduces the amount of data that needs to be processed."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 3:\n"""The rewrite rule applies semi-join optimizations to reduce result set size early in query processing. It transforms the query by discarding non-matching rows in the outer query earlier, possibly by pulling relevant data into a temporary structure or scanning data in a manner that avoids processing duplicate information unnecessarily. The rule also utilizes anti-joins and general JOIN optimizations to leverage database optimizations for JOIN operations, which might include better use of indexes and optimized data access paths."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 4:\n"""The rewrite rule replaces `IN` with `INTERSECT` for querying intersecting datasets to potentially improve index usage and query speed. It rewrites conditions using the OR operator into a series of UNION ALL operations to enhance code maintainability and performance. The rule also uses EXCEPT instead of NOT IN or anti-joins to minimize duplicate row processing and optimize resource use, effectively reducing execution time."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 5:\n"""The rewrite rule optimizes the selection of conditional attributes by integrating `GROUP BY` with aggregate functions like `MAX` within `CASE` statements, thus condensing the result set to only the necessary data and avoiding needless retrieval and processing steps. The rule combines multiple joins into a single join operation by using `CASE` statements to conditionally select different attributes from the table in one pass."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 6:\n"""The rewrite rule simplifies constant expressions upfront by transforming `(a<b AND b=c) AND a=5` into `b>5 AND b=c AND a=5`. It eliminates redundant or tautological conditions and evaluates constant expressions a singular time, improving the efficiency of indexed queries."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 7:\n"""The rewrite rule handles out-of-range constant values by either discarding the condition or converting it to a simpler form that reflects the overflow situation. It also adjusts precision for floating-point types to avoid precision errors."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 8:\n"""The rewrite rule applies derived condition pushdown by moving outer query conditions into subqueries without affecting the result. It also transforms conditions in the outer query not involving `GROUP BY` columns of a derived table with aggregation into the `HAVING` clause."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 9:\n"""The rewrite rule incorporates excessive selection criteria into subqueries or at initial stages of the main query to utilize indexes and minimize join operation sizes. It applies the most restrictive conditions early in the query to reduce the dataset size before executing joins and more complex operations."""\n\nQuery Rewrite 10:\n"""The rewrite rule applies derived condition pushdown by moving outer query conditions into subqueries without affecting the result. It also transforms conditions in the outer query not involving `GROUP BY` columns of a derived table with aggregation into the `HAVING` clause."""'}], 'model': 'llama3:8b', 'max_tokens': 1024, 'stream': False, 'temperature': 0.1}}
